---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import RootLayout from "../../layouts/RootLayout.astro";
import ProgramPage from "../../layouts/ProgramPage.astro";
import { sideBarForPrograms } from "../../constants.ts";
import { sideBarConstants } from "../../constants.ts";

const culturalResourcesPosts = await getCollection(
  "programPost",
  ({ data }) => {
    return data.programArea.includes("Cultural Resources");
  }
);

const pageTitle = "Cultural Resources";

const culturalResourcesAreas = [
  {
    title: "Regulations, Guidance, and Policy",
    children: [
      { title: "National Laws and Statutes" },
      { title: "National Executive Orders" },
      { title: "International Agreements" },
      { title: "Federal Agency Policy" },
    ],
  },

  {
    title: "Supporting Information and Tools",
    children: [
      { title: "Databases and Software Tools" },
      { title: "Directories, Catalogs, and Newsletters" },
      { title: "Libraries and Repositories" },
      { title: "Organizations and Programs" },
    ],
  },

  {
    title: "Lessons Learned",
    children: [
      { title: "Award Winners" },
      { title: "Case Studies" },
      { title: "Implementation Guidance" },
    ],
  },

  { title: "Training, Presentations, and Briefings" },
  { title: "Conferences and Events" },
];
---

<ProgramPage
  programAreas={culturalResourcesAreas}
  posts={culturalResourcesPosts}
  pageTitle={pageTitle}
>
  <p>
    The Cultural Resources Program Area addresses the background and available
    tools and resources for the management of cultural and historic resources.
    This includes: preservation of historic properties, archaeological sites,
    gravesites, repatriation of Native American objects, and curation of
    collections.
  </p>
  <p>
    The history of officially preserving historic properties, monuments,
    archeological sites, and museum displays dates back to the <a
      href="https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?path=/prelim@title54/subtitle3/divisionC&edition=prelim"
      target="new"
    >
      <i> American Antiquities Act of 1906 </i>
    </a> which authorized the President to declare historic landmarks, historic and
    prehistoric structures, and other objects of historic or scientific interest,
    that are situated upon the lands owned or controlled by the Federal Government,
    to be national monuments. It also authorized the Secretaries of the Interior (SOI),
    Agriculture, and Army to issue permits for the examination of ruins, the excavation
    of archaeological sites, and the gathering of objects of antiquity upon the lands
    under their respective jurisdictions.
  </p>
  <p>
    It was not until the <a
      href="https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?path=/prelim@title54/subtitle3/divisionA&edition=prelim"
      target="new"
    >
      <i> National Historic Preservation Act (NHPA) of 1966 </i>
    </a> that the goal of having federal agencies act as responsible stewards of our
    nation's resources when their actions affect historic properties became law. The
    NHPA also established the <a href="http://www.achp.gov/" target="new">
      Advisory Council on Historic Preservation (ACHP)
    </a>. The ACHP is the only entity with the legal responsibility to encourage
    federal agencies to factor historic preservation into federal project
    requirements. The ACHP serves as the primary federal policy advisor to the
    President and Congress; recommends administrative and legislative
    improvements for protecting our nation's heritage; advocates full
    consideration of historic values in federal decisionmaking; and reviews
    federal programs and policies to promote effectiveness, coordination, and
    consistency with national preservation policies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Interest in the management, preservation, and curation of archeological
    sites increased for federal agencies with the passage of the <a
      href="https://www.doi.gov/sites/doi.gov/files/uploads/ahpa.pdf"
      target="new"
    >
      <i> Archeological and Historic Preservation Act of 1974 (AHPA) </i>
    </a>, which requires Federal agencies to survey and recovery significant
    scientific, prehistorical, historical or archeological data that may be
    irreparably lost or destroyed during federal construction projects. In
    executing cultural resources responsibilities Federal facilities must often
    consult with their State Historic Preservation Officer, any potentially
    impacted Native American groups, and the responsible Department of Interior
    Bureaus and offices.
  </p>
</ProgramPage>
